1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The ancient world was not straight. It was not gay either. It was something far more complicated because most ancient civilizations did not divide humanity

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
into heterosexual, homosexual, bisexual, and transgender people. They divided people into citizens and foreigners,

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:25,000
free and enslaved, masculine and feminine, respectable and disgraceful, adult and youth, powerful and powerless.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Who you desired mattered, but who did what to whom and where each person stood in society often mattered much more.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:43,000
This creates a problem whenever someone announces that an ancient civilization accepted queer relationships. Accepted which relationship? Between two adult

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
citizens, between a wealthy man and an enslaved person who could not refuse.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
between women, between a ruler and a favorite, between a teacher and an adolescent, between people who crossed

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:04,000
gender roles. Was it celebrated, tolerated, mocked, taxed, restricted, or simply ignored?

9
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The answer can change before the sentence ends. Ancient Greece is usually presented as the obvious winner. Athens

10
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,000
had male lovers, homoerotic art, sappo, and a philosophical dinner party where men discussed love while drinking.

11
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Compared with many later societies, it can look like a rainbow paradise with columns. Then you inspect the rules.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Classical Greeks did not generally imagine sexual orientation as a fixed identity defining the entire person.

13
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Desire could be directed toward women or men without automatically placing someone into a permanent category equivalent to gay or bisexual.

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Greek literature and art openly acknowledged same-sex attraction, especially between males. It was not unspeakable. It could be praised as

15
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
beautiful, educational, heroic, or spiritually elevating. But acceptance was structured by age, status, and role.

16
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:06,000
In several Greek citystates, the best documented pattern involved an adult male citizen courting an adolescent male. The older partner was commonly

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
imagined as the active pursuer, while the younger was the admired beloved.

18
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Ideally, Greek writers framed the bond as more than sex. It could involve mentorship, education, military values,

19
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
gifts, status, and the cultivation of virtue. Ideally, is doing dangerous work in that sentence. The sources were

20
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
mostly written by elite men describing institutions that benefited elite men.

21
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The younger partner's experience is much harder to recover. The relationship was unequal by design, and ancient arguments

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
about proper age, restraint, consent, and exploitation show that Greeks themselves did not all treat every form

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:53,000
of it as admirable. Even in Athens, an adult male citizen was expected to protect his masculine public status.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Being seen as sexually passive could expose him to ridicule and political attack. A man could desire males and remain respectable, but his role had to

25
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:10,000
preserve the hierarchy. The social question was not merely, "Was your partner male?" It was, "Did this make you look subordinate?"

26
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Ancient Athens could praise male beauty while aggressively policing adult masculinity. It could place male male

27
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:26,000
desire inside philosophy and poetry while denying women political citizenship. It could discuss noble love between free males while allowing

28
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
citizens sexual access to enslaved people. This was not modern equality arriving early. It was a different system of inequality.

29
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Plato's symposium captures the attraction and the distance perfectly.

30
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Its speakers offer competing accounts of love. Aristophanes tells the famous myth that humans were once double beings

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
